(vector (map (:db/id -1)
 (:adr/id "adr-2609092000-a-build-input-is-addressed-by-its-content")
 (:adr/title "A build input is addressed by its content")
 (:adr/status "accepted")
 (:adr/date "2026-09-09")
 (:adr/authority "owner directive, 2026-09-09")
 (:adr/body "Owner directive, 2026-09-09, after asking whether kotoba-lang's build already
imports content-addressed libraries and how Unison, Nix and Deno prevent a bad
package: align the workspace to that premise.

## The premise

A build input is identified by the hash of its bytes, not by a name, a path or
a prefix of a hash. Anything fetched is verified against that hash before it is
used. A registry, a mirror or a cache is then untrusted by construction, which
is the property all four reference systems are actually buying.

## Where this workspace stood when the directive was given

Measured over 4,336 project files, 20,097 build inputs:

  :git/sha, 40 hex          6,954   addressed -- git hashes commit, tree, blobs
  :git/sha, 7 hex           3,675   A PREFIX. 2^28 is grindable and the :git/tag
                                    beside it is mutable. 3,673 are one value,
                                    io.github.cognitect-labs/test-runner's
                                    \"dfb30dd\", copied into nearly every deps.edn
  :mvn/version              6,317   a NAME. tools.deps has no integrity field in
                                    the coordinate, so nothing pins bytes
  :local/root, cross-repo   3,128   a PATH into another repo. Whatever is
                                    checked out is what gets built
  :local/root, same repo       19   fine -- one content unit either way
  deps-lock.edn                 1   in the whole workspace, in amu

So roughly two thirds of this workspace's build inputs are named rather than
addressed. amu is the exception and its lock is the right shape: coordinate,
url, sha, paths, plus a SHA-256 digest of deps.edn so a pin change fails closed.
That failure was observed today rather than assumed -- advancing amu's json pin
without regenerating the lock stopped the build with \"deps.edn changed since the
lock was generated\".

## What the reference systems do

Unison has no package. A definition is the hash of its typed AST with
dependencies already replaced by their hashes, so there is no name to confuse
and nothing to substitute. Names are metadata over hashes, which is also why
renaming costs nothing there and cost 11,900 call sites here.

Nix declares outputHash before fetching (fixed-output derivations) and derives
store paths from the full build recipe (input-addressed), so a substituter can
be untrusted: a wrong byte gives a wrong path or a failed hash check.

Deno's lock records a SHA-256 per remote module URL and per npm tarball, and
--frozen fails on drift. The unit is the fetched bytes.

Go adds what a per-project lock cannot: a transparency log, so a server cannot
serve different bytes to different people without it being detectable. That is
the first-fetch problem this workspace also has.

## The decision

Build inputs in this workspace are addressed by content, in this order, each
step measured rather than declared:

1. A :git/sha is written in full. A prefix is not an address. Mechanical; a wave
   is expanding the 3,673.
2. Every project that builds carries a lock in amu's shape, not one project.
   The lock is what holds the integrity a coordinate cannot.
3. :mvn/version gains a SHA-256 per artifact IN THE LOCK, since tools.deps has
   nowhere to put one.
4. A cross-repo :local/root gains a revision. 3,128 of them currently address
   nothing, and verify-source-path-pins already measured 240 of 383 shadow-cljs
   source paths sitting at a revision west.yml does not pin.
5. Fetched bytes are verified against the declared hash before use --
   kotoba.storage.verify/verifying-block-store already does exactly this for
   blocks, and the same seam covers dependency trees.
6. The first fetch is covered by a transparency log rather than trust on first
   use. inga's quorum certificates and the append-only ledger are that shape.

## What enforces it

scripts/verify-build-input-addressing.cljs, registered daily. It classifies
every coordinate and reports prefixes at :warn -- the one class that should
reach zero -- with maven and cross-repo paths as standing counts at :info, so
each number moves visibly when its step lands.

⚠ It measures the CHECKOUT, not the repos' mains. A wave that expands prefixes
everywhere will not move this number until west brings the checkouts along; a
count that has not moved is not evidence that nothing landed.

Discriminated three ways on fixture trees before it was registered: one prefix
among 601 inputs exits 1 and names it, the same tree with every sha in full
exits 0, and a tree below the evidence floor exits 2 saying it could not
measure.")
))
